WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.107
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:02.107 --> 00:00:03.690
DANIEL JACKSON: These
ethical concerns

00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:06.300
don't come about because
of simple binary choices

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:08.140
between being good or evil.

00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:11.670
They come about largely
because of lack of awareness.

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:13.200
SARAH HANSEN: Today
on Chalk Radio,

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:16.770
we're talking about how the
ethical decisions of engineers

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:19.195
shape the software
we use every day.

00:00:19.195 --> 00:00:20.820
SERENA BOOTH:
Ultimately, through going

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:23.250
through this concrete
process, the goal

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:27.060
is to get you to identify what
we call value-laden decision

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:30.990
points in your engineering
work and to choose better, just

00:00:30.990 --> 00:00:34.380
through being more
informed and deliberate.

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:36.540
SARAH HANSEN: I'm your
host, Sarah Hansen.

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:40.290
This week, we're in conversation
with two MIT scientists

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:43.500
who are thinking deeply about
how software design impacts

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:45.750
our experiences in the world.

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:47.490
They're also
encouraging students

00:00:47.490 --> 00:00:50.910
to examine the fault lines
between humans and technology

00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:54.000
and helping students to
consider the social and ethical

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:56.250
responsibilities of computing.

00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:59.543
My guests are Daniel
Jackson and Serena Booth.

00:00:59.543 --> 00:01:01.710
DANIEL JACKSON: I'm a
Professor of Computer Science,

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:05.850
and I'm also an Associate
Director of CSAIL, the Computer

00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:08.340
Science and AI Lab at MIT.

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:10.950
SERENA BOOTH: I'm entering my
fourth year as a PhD student

00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:13.033
here at MIT.

00:01:13.033 --> 00:01:15.200
SARAH HANSEN: Before
collaborating in the classroom,

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:17.710
Daniel and Serena were
both conducting research

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:20.350
at the intersection
of complex technology

00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:23.230
and everyday human experiences.

00:01:23.230 --> 00:01:26.470
DANIEL JACKSON: The focus of
my work in the last few years

00:01:26.470 --> 00:01:31.120
has been to try and
understand design of software

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:34.840
not from the point of view of
its internal structure, which

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:36.970
by the way, is what people
actually mean when they

00:01:36.970 --> 00:01:38.650
talk about designing software.

00:01:38.650 --> 00:01:40.840
They mean, how do
you write the code,

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:42.760
how do you organize
the code, how do you

00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:44.830
fit things together, and so on.

00:01:44.830 --> 00:01:47.860
But rather, design in the
sense that anyone out there

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:51.370
in the world would use
the term, namely design

00:01:51.370 --> 00:01:54.790
at the interface between
machine and human being.

00:01:54.790 --> 00:01:57.502
And I've become very
interested in the question of,

00:01:57.502 --> 00:01:58.960
essentially, whether
we can come up

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:01.690
with a kind of grand
theory of design

00:02:01.690 --> 00:02:04.780
that can explain how
to design software

00:02:04.780 --> 00:02:08.199
so that it's more usable, more
secure, more safe, and more

00:02:08.199 --> 00:02:09.430
pleasant.

00:02:09.430 --> 00:02:11.350
SERENA BOOTH: I
really want to create

00:02:11.350 --> 00:02:15.050
useful general-purpose robots
which collaborate with people.

00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:18.010
So it's important to me that
robots don't replace people,

00:02:18.010 --> 00:02:20.590
they accentuate the things
that we like in our lives

00:02:20.590 --> 00:02:23.110
and take away some of the
chores and the boring things.

00:02:23.110 --> 00:02:26.560
I think we often forget
about the need for the person

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:28.810
to work and collaborate
with that robot.

00:02:28.810 --> 00:02:30.730
That's where I'm
really interested,

00:02:30.730 --> 00:02:34.630
trying to get humans to
understand how robots work

00:02:34.630 --> 00:02:37.720
to be able to predict what
they'll do next so that you can

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:43.070
make this collaboration safe and
useful to both human and robot.

00:02:43.070 --> 00:02:45.380
SARAH HANSEN: As they've
pursued their own research,

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:48.200
they've both witnessed how
different kinds of seemingly

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:52.130
simple design decisions have
serious social and ethical

00:02:52.130 --> 00:02:53.780
implications.

00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:56.120
DANIEL JACKSON:
I would say I can

00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:59.610
put them very crudely into
three different categories.

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:05.300
The first is that it's very
easy to design and build

00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:08.240
a device or a system
or an application that

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:12.500
simply doesn't fulfill
its intended purpose well.

00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:14.210
A really compelling
example of this,

00:03:14.210 --> 00:03:18.920
for me, is the panoply of
medical devices often designed

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:20.810
by companies that
have great technology

00:03:20.810 --> 00:03:23.630
but don't have sufficient
expertise in software

00:03:23.630 --> 00:03:25.490
and human-computer
interaction design

00:03:25.490 --> 00:03:29.150
to think about questions related
to what kinds of mistakes

00:03:29.150 --> 00:03:30.090
the users can make.

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:33.290
So there are many horrendous
stories of infusion pumps,

00:03:33.290 --> 00:03:38.030
for example, in which nurses
enter incorrect dosage rates,

00:03:38.030 --> 00:03:41.750
and people are killed
or severely injured

00:03:41.750 --> 00:03:45.140
because, for example, a
chemotherapy drug is delivered

00:03:45.140 --> 00:03:48.420
at much too high a concentration
or much too high a volume.

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:50.750
But medical devices
are just one category

00:03:50.750 --> 00:03:55.010
of systems that are often poorly
implemented in a way that,

00:03:55.010 --> 00:03:57.530
essentially, doesn't
appreciate how important it

00:03:57.530 --> 00:04:00.920
is to accommodate the kinds
of errors that users make.

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:04.760
And I should say, there's also a
well known and very unfortunate

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:09.200
tendency in society to blame
users for the errors they make.

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:11.980
As just one example,
a horrendous accident,

00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:15.290
a series of accidents, due to a
radiotherapy machine in Panama

00:04:15.290 --> 00:04:18.352
led to all the nurses involved
in it being prosecuted

00:04:18.352 --> 00:04:20.060
and, I think, some of
them going to jail,

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:23.130
despite the fact that it was
clearly a system design flaw.

00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:25.130
So that's the first
category, things not meeting

00:04:25.130 --> 00:04:26.100
their purposes.

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:29.480
The second category is
that they are designed

00:04:29.480 --> 00:04:31.850
to meet certain purposes,
but those purposes

00:04:31.850 --> 00:04:34.060
are insidious ones.

00:04:34.060 --> 00:04:37.030
I'll give you an example of
one which is insidious only

00:04:37.030 --> 00:04:40.000
in a mild way, and that is
that a lot of applications

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:42.250
provide some kind of
notification feature.

00:04:42.250 --> 00:04:45.580
And you might imagine that's to
serve what the word suggests,

00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:47.140
to notify the users.

00:04:47.140 --> 00:04:49.450
So Facebook tells you, for
example, when there might

00:04:49.450 --> 00:04:51.700
be posts of interest to you.

00:04:51.700 --> 00:04:53.530
But if you dig a
little deeper, and you

00:04:53.530 --> 00:04:57.640
look into the question of
how easily you can actually

00:04:57.640 --> 00:05:00.070
configure those settings
and decide what you want

00:05:00.070 --> 00:05:02.860
to be notified about, you'll
discover that, in fact, you

00:05:02.860 --> 00:05:04.190
have very little control.

00:05:04.190 --> 00:05:07.000
And what that reveals
is that the real purpose

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:11.560
of this notification
concept is, in fact,

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:13.660
to encourage
engagement in the site

00:05:13.660 --> 00:05:17.710
and to draw people to visit
more frequently than is actually

00:05:17.710 --> 00:05:18.610
good for them.

00:05:18.610 --> 00:05:19.960
That's the second category.

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:23.470
The third category are
basically the respects

00:05:23.470 --> 00:05:29.290
in which designs can contribute
to a computational environment

00:05:29.290 --> 00:05:31.240
that has subtly
pernicious effects.

00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:33.040
And those effects
include, for example,

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:34.930
marginalizing certain
groups of people,

00:05:34.930 --> 00:05:37.240
advantaging some
users over others,

00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:40.840
encouraging antisocial or
undemocratic behaviors,

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:45.070
creating damage to community,
or even just personally reducing

00:05:45.070 --> 00:05:48.610
quality of life by making us
less reflective or creative

00:05:48.610 --> 00:05:51.460
or independent by enslaving
us to our devices,

00:05:51.460 --> 00:05:53.950
by sapping our attention,
and all these things

00:05:53.950 --> 00:05:57.130
that we've all become very
concerned with with the devices

00:05:57.130 --> 00:05:58.960
we use but which
are not necessarily

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:01.990
inherent in those devices
but exacerbated by the design

00:06:01.990 --> 00:06:04.812
decisions we make.

00:06:04.812 --> 00:06:07.020
SERENA BOOTH: I can give
you an anecdote from my days

00:06:07.020 --> 00:06:07.720
at Google.

00:06:07.720 --> 00:06:09.990
So I'd just graduated
from college.

00:06:09.990 --> 00:06:11.430
I was, like, 22 years old.

00:06:11.430 --> 00:06:13.530
I'd been at Google
for a few weeks,

00:06:13.530 --> 00:06:15.030
and I was working on Search.

00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:18.630
And my team came to me, and
we were having this crisis.

00:06:18.630 --> 00:06:21.990
We were redesigning the
dinosaurs search page.

00:06:21.990 --> 00:06:23.700
And everyone wasn't sure.

00:06:23.700 --> 00:06:27.330
We'd gotten a lot of bugs
from parents complaining

00:06:27.330 --> 00:06:30.930
about our dinosaurs search page
because "pterodactyl" didn't

00:06:30.930 --> 00:06:33.520
appear there, and their
children wanted it there.

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:35.820
And so we had this
little crisis as a team.

00:06:35.820 --> 00:06:38.610
We're like, should we put
"pterodactyl" on there?

00:06:38.610 --> 00:06:39.780
It's not really a dinosaur.

00:06:39.780 --> 00:06:41.400
It's technically
a pterosaur, so we

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:42.930
would be lying to the children.

00:06:42.930 --> 00:06:45.370
And because I was
a product manager,

00:06:45.370 --> 00:06:47.050
they were like, OK,
Serena, you decide.

00:06:47.050 --> 00:06:49.620
And so I just made a decision,
and then we moved on.

00:06:49.620 --> 00:06:53.100
And this is like totally
unimportant, right?

00:06:53.100 --> 00:06:54.990
It doesn't really
matter if the kids think

00:06:54.990 --> 00:06:56.430
pterodactyl is a dinosaur.

00:06:56.430 --> 00:06:59.580
But it just opens the
door, the floodgates,

00:06:59.580 --> 00:07:03.570
to thinking about how much
control we have over how people

00:07:03.570 --> 00:07:04.810
understand the world.

00:07:04.810 --> 00:07:06.690
And so, yeah, those
sorts of decisions

00:07:06.690 --> 00:07:09.565
are things I think about a lot.

00:07:09.565 --> 00:07:11.190
SARAH HANSEN: Serena
and Daniel noticed

00:07:11.190 --> 00:07:14.370
a gap between the questions
they were asking themselves

00:07:14.370 --> 00:07:17.040
as researchers and what
students were being

00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:19.233
asked inside the classroom.

00:07:19.233 --> 00:07:20.900
DANIEL JACKSON: I'd
always been troubled

00:07:20.900 --> 00:07:23.960
by the way in which
we had perhaps

00:07:23.960 --> 00:07:26.540
not only allowed
but even encouraged

00:07:26.540 --> 00:07:29.930
our students to marginalize
ethical concerns

00:07:29.930 --> 00:07:32.780
and sometimes think of
them in rather trite terms.

00:07:32.780 --> 00:07:36.230
One thing that always comes
to mind to me is that, when.

00:07:36.230 --> 00:07:40.710
Google was founded, there was
this slogan, "Do no evil."

00:07:40.710 --> 00:07:43.550
And there was this sense, I
think, amongst our students

00:07:43.550 --> 00:07:45.680
and amongst a lot of
young technologists

00:07:45.680 --> 00:07:47.260
that we're good people.

00:07:47.260 --> 00:07:49.010
And so it's understandable
that we thought

00:07:49.010 --> 00:07:50.240
we didn't want to do evil.

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:53.930
But the truth is, most
people don't want to do evil.

00:07:53.930 --> 00:07:56.930
The people in charge
of the companies that

00:07:56.930 --> 00:07:59.870
do some of the most terrible
things to the planet

00:07:59.870 --> 00:08:01.250
are really not evil people.

00:08:01.250 --> 00:08:05.000
Most of them are people who love
their families and friends, who

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:06.500
want to do good in the world.

00:08:06.500 --> 00:08:09.440
And these ethical
concerns don't come about

00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:12.900
because of simple binary choices
between being good or evil.

00:08:12.900 --> 00:08:15.480
They come about largely
because of lack of awareness.

00:08:15.480 --> 00:08:17.490
And as we've seen with
Google, for example,

00:08:17.490 --> 00:08:18.950
money corrupts everything.

00:08:18.950 --> 00:08:22.130
And once you start to be
hugely financially successful,

00:08:22.130 --> 00:08:24.500
unless you have a very
strong ethical lens,

00:08:24.500 --> 00:08:28.100
it's very hard to resist
the temptations of money.

00:08:28.100 --> 00:08:29.570
And I thought it
was very important

00:08:29.570 --> 00:08:33.140
for us to be able to inoculate
our students against some

00:08:33.140 --> 00:08:34.940
of those temptations
and for them

00:08:34.940 --> 00:08:37.100
to develop the kind
of sophistication

00:08:37.100 --> 00:08:39.650
in their social and ethical
understanding of the world

00:08:39.650 --> 00:08:42.320
that they have in their
technological understanding.

00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:45.440
I will say, also, there was a
very simple pragmatic reason

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:47.600
that I wanted to be
involved with SERC,

00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:51.140
which was that I didn't
know how to do this myself.

00:08:51.140 --> 00:08:53.180
SARAH HANSEN: You may
remember the acronym SERC

00:08:53.180 --> 00:08:56.390
from our previous episode with
Catherine D'Ignazio, Jacob

00:08:56.390 --> 00:08:58.610
Andreas, and Harini Suresh.

00:08:58.610 --> 00:09:03.060
It stands for Social and Ethical
Responsibilities of Computing.

00:09:03.060 --> 00:09:06.140
SERENA BOOTH: So recently,
MIT announced its launch

00:09:06.140 --> 00:09:09.140
of the College of Computing,
which is a new endeavor

00:09:09.140 --> 00:09:12.230
to try to bring
cross-disciplinary computing

00:09:12.230 --> 00:09:13.310
all together.

00:09:13.310 --> 00:09:16.250
It's going to really
change MIT as we know it.

00:09:16.250 --> 00:09:19.190
But as part of that, also,
they've launched SERC.

00:09:19.190 --> 00:09:22.430
The goal is to bring all of
the different perspectives

00:09:22.430 --> 00:09:25.670
on social and ethical
responsibilities together--

00:09:25.670 --> 00:09:29.030
so philosophers,
social scientists,

00:09:29.030 --> 00:09:32.240
cognitive scientists, computer
scientists, many more--

00:09:32.240 --> 00:09:37.160
and get that coalition of people
to help embed ethics education

00:09:37.160 --> 00:09:40.610
into MIT's curriculum,
very broadly.

00:09:40.610 --> 00:09:42.440
SARAH HANSEN: In
collaboration with SERC,

00:09:42.440 --> 00:09:45.560
Serena joined Daniel as a
graduate teaching assistant

00:09:45.560 --> 00:09:50.390
for the course 6.170
Software Studio.

00:09:50.390 --> 00:09:52.610
One of their primary
goals was to bring

00:09:52.610 --> 00:09:54.890
social and ethical
considerations

00:09:54.890 --> 00:09:57.980
into the engineering classroom.

00:09:57.980 --> 00:10:01.060
That's when they turned to a
powerful tool called the Ethics

00:10:01.060 --> 00:10:01.875
Protocol.

00:10:01.875 --> 00:10:03.250
SERENA BOOTH: The
Ethics Protocol

00:10:03.250 --> 00:10:04.900
was developed here at MIT.

00:10:04.900 --> 00:10:07.030
It was developed by
Milo Phillips-Brown,

00:10:07.030 --> 00:10:10.570
who's now an assistant
professor at Oxford University.

00:10:10.570 --> 00:10:11.950
And Abby Jaques.

00:10:11.950 --> 00:10:14.320
They are both
philosophers by training,

00:10:14.320 --> 00:10:16.480
and as they matured
in their careers,

00:10:16.480 --> 00:10:17.980
they started to
get more involved

00:10:17.980 --> 00:10:21.280
in designing ethics-related
materials for computing,

00:10:21.280 --> 00:10:22.670
specifically.

00:10:22.670 --> 00:10:25.210
So the Ethics Protocol
is a tool which

00:10:25.210 --> 00:10:27.400
tries to assist in
their mission, which

00:10:27.400 --> 00:10:29.620
is to teach ethics as a skill.

00:10:29.620 --> 00:10:32.920
Ultimately, through going
through this concrete process,

00:10:32.920 --> 00:10:34.930
the goal is to get
you to identify

00:10:34.930 --> 00:10:37.120
what we call
value-laden decision

00:10:37.120 --> 00:10:40.450
points in your engineering
work and to choose better

00:10:40.450 --> 00:10:43.888
just through being more
informed and deliberate.

00:10:43.888 --> 00:10:45.430
SARAH HANSEN: Daniel
and Serena noted

00:10:45.430 --> 00:10:48.820
that it's not possible or
ethical to simply give students

00:10:48.820 --> 00:10:52.660
a singular ethical principle
to guide their decision making.

00:10:52.660 --> 00:10:56.290
Instead students are introduced
to a set of three lenses

00:10:56.290 --> 00:10:59.890
that they can apply when
assessing a project or idea.

00:10:59.890 --> 00:11:02.110
The three lenses of
the Ethics Protocol

00:11:02.110 --> 00:11:05.440
are outcomes,
structure, and process.

00:11:05.440 --> 00:11:07.120
SERENA BOOTH: The
outcomes lens asks

00:11:07.120 --> 00:11:10.780
you to analyze the consequences
of a decision or intervention,

00:11:10.780 --> 00:11:12.940
and that's a very
intuitive one to use.

00:11:12.940 --> 00:11:15.970
The process lens asks you
to consider the treatment

00:11:15.970 --> 00:11:17.300
of your stakeholders.

00:11:17.300 --> 00:11:19.690
So you should think about
things like your stakeholders'

00:11:19.690 --> 00:11:22.790
autonomy, their consent,
their participation.

00:11:22.790 --> 00:11:24.880
And then that last lens,
the structure lens,

00:11:24.880 --> 00:11:28.750
asks you to analyze how outcomes
and process is distributed

00:11:28.750 --> 00:11:30.350
among the stakeholders.

00:11:30.350 --> 00:11:33.640
Another good and perhaps more
descriptive name for this lens

00:11:33.640 --> 00:11:35.440
would be a justice lens.

00:11:35.440 --> 00:11:37.830
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:11:37.830 --> 00:11:40.560
So in software
design, we typically

00:11:40.560 --> 00:11:43.440
have one definition for
stakeholders, somebody

00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:46.680
who's involved in
making, using, or would

00:11:46.680 --> 00:11:48.690
be likely to use your software.

00:11:48.690 --> 00:11:51.450
You're building for a
relatively small set of people,

00:11:51.450 --> 00:11:53.820
and you're trying to be
very mindful of their needs

00:11:53.820 --> 00:11:55.020
as you build.

00:11:55.020 --> 00:11:58.440
But when you think instead
about ethical implications,

00:11:58.440 --> 00:12:00.180
scoping your
stakeholders this way

00:12:00.180 --> 00:12:02.460
is just not reasonable, right?

00:12:02.460 --> 00:12:05.220
You should also consider
all of the stakeholders who

00:12:05.220 --> 00:12:08.160
are affected or could be
affected by your software.

00:12:08.160 --> 00:12:09.810
As a concrete
example, perhaps we

00:12:09.810 --> 00:12:12.480
could think about a
company like Uber or Lyft.

00:12:12.480 --> 00:12:15.900
So under the software
definition of stakeholders,

00:12:15.900 --> 00:12:19.170
you probably think about
drivers and customers, right?

00:12:19.170 --> 00:12:22.170
Those are the only two
stakeholders who really matter.

00:12:22.170 --> 00:12:25.302
But under the Ethics Protocol
definition of stakeholders,

00:12:25.302 --> 00:12:26.760
you should think
about others, too.

00:12:26.760 --> 00:12:31.050
So the very obvious example
here is traditional taxi drivers

00:12:31.050 --> 00:12:34.490
who are being undercut
by these apps.

00:12:34.490 --> 00:12:37.760
It's impossible for
you to think of like

00:12:37.760 --> 00:12:42.110
every possible stakeholder
and every potential effect

00:12:42.110 --> 00:12:43.520
that your software could have.

00:12:43.520 --> 00:12:45.800
It's just not tenable, right?

00:12:45.800 --> 00:12:49.040
And so there are things that
will be out of your control,

00:12:49.040 --> 00:12:50.640
and you have to be OK with that.

00:12:50.640 --> 00:12:52.400
But nonetheless,
you should still

00:12:52.400 --> 00:12:55.790
try to anticipate as
much as possible to avoid

00:12:55.790 --> 00:12:59.852
those unintended
consequences downstream.

00:12:59.852 --> 00:13:01.560
SARAH HANSEN: I was
curious what this all

00:13:01.560 --> 00:13:03.660
looks like in the
classroom, when

00:13:03.660 --> 00:13:07.920
students begin to put the
Ethics Protocol into practice.

00:13:07.920 --> 00:13:09.840
DANIEL JACKSON:
Our course consists

00:13:09.840 --> 00:13:11.200
of, essentially, two parts.

00:13:11.200 --> 00:13:12.900
In the first part
of the term, they're

00:13:12.900 --> 00:13:16.290
learning the basic design ideas
and the basic technologies

00:13:16.290 --> 00:13:18.540
and putting them together
and applying them

00:13:18.540 --> 00:13:20.640
on small,
well-contained problems.

00:13:20.640 --> 00:13:22.740
And then in the second
half of the term,

00:13:22.740 --> 00:13:24.960
they join together
in teams, and they

00:13:24.960 --> 00:13:27.600
take on a larger project
of their own invention.

00:13:27.600 --> 00:13:30.060
And they build that
project from concept

00:13:30.060 --> 00:13:32.940
to implementation and
deployment and everything.

00:13:32.940 --> 00:13:37.110
We introduce these social and
ethical concerns incrementally.

00:13:37.110 --> 00:13:39.660
We start with the
individual assignments

00:13:39.660 --> 00:13:43.710
by asking them to reflect
on the ethical and social

00:13:43.710 --> 00:13:47.340
ramifications of the
assignment they're doing

00:13:47.340 --> 00:13:49.290
and the implications
of that assignment.

00:13:49.290 --> 00:13:52.590
And we slowly ramp
up the intensity

00:13:52.590 --> 00:13:56.350
and the sophistication of the
analysis that we ask from them.

00:13:56.350 --> 00:13:59.820
And then, as we move
towards the final project

00:13:59.820 --> 00:14:02.970
we ask them to begin to take
those decisions into account

00:14:02.970 --> 00:14:04.470
in their own design work.

00:14:04.470 --> 00:14:06.750
And then in the
final project, we

00:14:06.750 --> 00:14:09.930
encourage them to
develop their ideas

00:14:09.930 --> 00:14:12.930
using these lenses
that Serena described

00:14:12.930 --> 00:14:15.235
and in the context of
this kind of analysis.

00:14:15.235 --> 00:14:17.610
SERENA BOOTH: And it was part
of their deliverable, which

00:14:17.610 --> 00:14:19.060
I think is really important.

00:14:19.060 --> 00:14:21.540
So it wasn't just
them talking about it.

00:14:21.540 --> 00:14:23.110
It was part of their grade.

00:14:23.110 --> 00:14:25.410
And it felt very
real and important.

00:14:25.410 --> 00:14:29.340
And I think we saw the students
really participate and start

00:14:29.340 --> 00:14:32.220
to integrate this into
their thinking, partially

00:14:32.220 --> 00:14:35.550
as a consequence of
that integration.

00:14:35.550 --> 00:14:38.380
DANIEL JACKSON: It was
actually a surprise even to me,

00:14:38.380 --> 00:14:42.210
despite my enthusiasm for
this material, that we found

00:14:42.210 --> 00:14:46.080
that there were so many social
and ethical concerns even

00:14:46.080 --> 00:14:49.710
in the relatively narrow
context of the rather technical

00:14:49.710 --> 00:14:51.390
assignments at the beginning.

00:14:51.390 --> 00:14:53.100
So I'll just give
you one example.

00:14:53.100 --> 00:14:56.730
We have them build
what we call Fritter.

00:14:56.730 --> 00:14:58.740
Fritter is, essentially,
a Twitter clone.

00:14:58.740 --> 00:15:02.230
But just questions
regarding, for example,

00:15:02.230 --> 00:15:04.980
what kinds of posts are
shown, what the criteria are

00:15:04.980 --> 00:15:08.550
for displaying posts, tweets,
or freets, as we call them,

00:15:08.550 --> 00:15:10.590
what order they
appear in, and so on

00:15:10.590 --> 00:15:14.030
can have social ramifications.

00:15:14.030 --> 00:15:16.030
SARAH HANSEN: At this
point in the conversation,

00:15:16.030 --> 00:15:19.450
I started wondering what
it was like to teach ethics

00:15:19.450 --> 00:15:23.470
in the context of
cancel culture.

00:15:23.470 --> 00:15:25.410
But as Daniel and
Serena explained,

00:15:25.410 --> 00:15:28.260
students aren't being
taught what to think.

00:15:28.260 --> 00:15:31.080
They aren't being asked to
espouse particular ideologies

00:15:31.080 --> 00:15:32.160
in the classroom.

00:15:32.160 --> 00:15:36.210
Instead, they're learning
how to ask better questions.

00:15:36.210 --> 00:15:38.100
SERENA BOOTH: We try
not to be prescriptive.

00:15:38.100 --> 00:15:39.570
Sometimes that
can be quite hard,

00:15:39.570 --> 00:15:41.250
because we all have
quite a strong sense

00:15:41.250 --> 00:15:42.210
of right and wrong.

00:15:42.210 --> 00:15:44.850
But instead, what we're
trying to do mostly

00:15:44.850 --> 00:15:47.940
is to get the students
to question the decisions

00:15:47.940 --> 00:15:51.750
that they're making and to
try to refine and iterate

00:15:51.750 --> 00:15:54.360
on their own moral
and ethical compasses.

00:15:54.360 --> 00:15:57.360
And so we want them to
develop that understanding

00:15:57.360 --> 00:15:59.490
for themselves
through their own lens

00:15:59.490 --> 00:16:02.670
and leave MIT with
that knowledge.

00:16:02.670 --> 00:16:05.010
DANIEL JACKSON: Just because
something is subjective,

00:16:05.010 --> 00:16:09.330
just because we might not
all agree on evaluation

00:16:09.330 --> 00:16:12.060
of particular
ethical consequences

00:16:12.060 --> 00:16:15.000
doesn't mean that
we can't attain

00:16:15.000 --> 00:16:17.610
some kind of objective
understanding of what it means

00:16:17.610 --> 00:16:21.840
to have a competent ethical
analysis that is appropriately

00:16:21.840 --> 00:16:25.795
far-reaching, balanced, and
deepened in different respects.

00:16:25.795 --> 00:16:28.170
One of the things that we're
trying to teach the students

00:16:28.170 --> 00:16:31.500
is that this isn't about any
particular ideology or dogma.

00:16:31.500 --> 00:16:35.910
As Serena explained, what we do
is we really give them tools.

00:16:35.910 --> 00:16:38.010
And we provide them
with these lenses.

00:16:38.010 --> 00:16:41.760
And we never suggest that
any one ethical evaluation

00:16:41.760 --> 00:16:44.460
is correct and another
one is incorrect.

00:16:44.460 --> 00:16:47.850
We don't say that Uber should
make its decisions according

00:16:47.850 --> 00:16:52.470
to the impact on the livelihood
of existing taxi drivers.

00:16:52.470 --> 00:16:56.250
Nor do we say that the
shareholders of Uber

00:16:56.250 --> 00:16:58.290
are the most important
stakeholders.

00:16:58.290 --> 00:17:01.020
Nor do we say that the
drivers or the passengers

00:17:01.020 --> 00:17:01.990
are important.

00:17:01.990 --> 00:17:04.829
The point is that
any design decision

00:17:04.829 --> 00:17:06.990
must be made with a
full awareness of which

00:17:06.990 --> 00:17:10.210
stakeholders you're actually
taking into account.

00:17:10.210 --> 00:17:13.180
And then how you actually
do that yourself,

00:17:13.180 --> 00:17:15.869
well, that's of course,
tremendously important.

00:17:15.869 --> 00:17:19.230
We're not for a minute
suggesting to the students

00:17:19.230 --> 00:17:23.220
that there's any kind of
predefined ethical judgments

00:17:23.220 --> 00:17:24.609
that we expect them to make.

00:17:24.609 --> 00:17:26.026
And on the contrary,
I think, we'd

00:17:26.026 --> 00:17:29.010
actually like to complicate
their understanding.

00:17:29.010 --> 00:17:32.070
My goal is always to
persuade the students

00:17:32.070 --> 00:17:33.810
and myself the
preconceptions that we

00:17:33.810 --> 00:17:37.290
come in with are
too simple-minded

00:17:37.290 --> 00:17:40.200
and that there's a lot of work
to be done to understand things

00:17:40.200 --> 00:17:42.730
more deeply.

00:17:42.730 --> 00:17:45.190
SARAH HANSEN: As they develop
these deeper understandings,

00:17:45.190 --> 00:17:48.400
students in Daniel and
Serena's Software Studio course

00:17:48.400 --> 00:17:51.670
are also challenging stereotypes
about the kind of work

00:17:51.670 --> 00:17:54.558
engineering students
can be passionate about.

00:17:54.558 --> 00:17:56.350
DANIEL JACKSON: Our
students, it turns out,

00:17:56.350 --> 00:18:00.250
are absolutely passionate about
making their mark in the world

00:18:00.250 --> 00:18:02.870
and about being part
of a larger community.

00:18:02.870 --> 00:18:06.130
I think it's just a question
of giving them the opportunity

00:18:06.130 --> 00:18:06.850
to do this.

00:18:06.850 --> 00:18:09.490
I suspect if you
asked them, "Would you

00:18:09.490 --> 00:18:12.310
like to take a course on social
and ethical ramifications

00:18:12.310 --> 00:18:14.247
of computing?" they
might not list it.

00:18:14.247 --> 00:18:16.330
It's actually very much
parallel to the experience

00:18:16.330 --> 00:18:20.590
of the humanities at MIT, where
students generally don't rush

00:18:20.590 --> 00:18:22.243
towards the humanities classes.

00:18:22.243 --> 00:18:24.910
They're required to take some as
part of their General Institute

00:18:24.910 --> 00:18:25.720
Requirements.

00:18:25.720 --> 00:18:27.850
But when they take
those classes,

00:18:27.850 --> 00:18:29.530
typically they love them.

00:18:29.530 --> 00:18:31.420
And they love them not
only because they're

00:18:31.420 --> 00:18:34.750
a break from the much more
sometimes concrete and narrow

00:18:34.750 --> 00:18:37.030
thinking that they do in
their technical classes,

00:18:37.030 --> 00:18:38.560
but I think because
it allows them

00:18:38.560 --> 00:18:40.930
to bring their whole
selves to their work.

00:18:40.930 --> 00:18:43.540
And it makes them feel
that they have agency

00:18:43.540 --> 00:18:45.940
and that they are
actually contributing

00:18:45.940 --> 00:18:49.013
in a broader and deeper way.

00:18:49.013 --> 00:18:50.680
SARAH HANSEN: It isn't
just the students

00:18:50.680 --> 00:18:53.710
who are being prompted to think
more deeply and creatively

00:18:53.710 --> 00:18:55.840
about engineering coursework.

00:18:55.840 --> 00:18:57.790
SERENA BOOTH: So I think
one of the challenges

00:18:57.790 --> 00:19:02.290
in embedding SERC materials is
that the university experience

00:19:02.290 --> 00:19:03.670
is like very much a bubble.

00:19:03.670 --> 00:19:06.820
Normally, you're working
on kind of toy problems.

00:19:06.820 --> 00:19:08.830
You're not shipping
software, right?

00:19:08.830 --> 00:19:11.050
Your decisions
kind of don't have

00:19:11.050 --> 00:19:15.190
social and ethical implications,
because probably your software

00:19:15.190 --> 00:19:17.670
isn't going to leave
your hands, really.

00:19:17.670 --> 00:19:18.520
Your TA will see it.

00:19:18.520 --> 00:19:19.750
Your professor will see it.

00:19:19.750 --> 00:19:22.570
But it's still
very closely held.

00:19:22.570 --> 00:19:24.880
So something that
I struggle with

00:19:24.880 --> 00:19:28.240
is trying to bridge this
gap to having the decisions

00:19:28.240 --> 00:19:31.730
that you make have real
world consequences.

00:19:31.730 --> 00:19:33.640
And so in this class,
the way we tried

00:19:33.640 --> 00:19:36.910
to approach it was by
making it realistic.

00:19:36.910 --> 00:19:41.290
But it's still, to some extent,
like building in a sandbox.

00:19:41.290 --> 00:19:44.140
And so I'm curious about
what other educators do

00:19:44.140 --> 00:19:47.650
to try to make this extremely
real to your students, right?

00:19:47.650 --> 00:19:50.770
How can we make the social
and ethical responsibilities

00:19:50.770 --> 00:19:51.885
questions tangible?

00:19:51.885 --> 00:19:53.260
DANIEL JACKSON:
The other thing I

00:19:53.260 --> 00:19:55.343
would say that, for me,
is a very big challenge is

00:19:55.343 --> 00:19:58.480
that we're constantly feeling
overwhelmed by the amount

00:19:58.480 --> 00:20:01.240
of technical material
that we need to teach.

00:20:01.240 --> 00:20:04.240
And we're finding
ways to reduce that

00:20:04.240 --> 00:20:07.300
by preparing the students
better in earlier courses,

00:20:07.300 --> 00:20:09.280
by flipping the classroom more.

00:20:09.280 --> 00:20:11.770
But I would welcome
any ideas that people

00:20:11.770 --> 00:20:16.390
have on how to switch students
out of this mindset, that just

00:20:16.390 --> 00:20:19.990
learning the details of the
programming language or the web

00:20:19.990 --> 00:20:23.200
platform that they're using or
whatever is all that matters

00:20:23.200 --> 00:20:25.930
and to get them to
see beyond that.

00:20:25.930 --> 00:20:28.618
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:20:29.423 --> 00:20:30.840
SARAH HANSEN: If
you have insights

00:20:30.840 --> 00:20:33.750
to share about teaching social
and ethical responsibilities

00:20:33.750 --> 00:20:36.000
of computing, please
get in touch with me

00:20:36.000 --> 00:20:37.830
through the link
in our show notes.

00:20:37.830 --> 00:20:40.710
I'll pass your ideas
along to Daniel, Serena,

00:20:40.710 --> 00:20:42.450
and other listeners.

00:20:42.450 --> 00:20:45.270
If you're interested in learning
more about ethics and software

00:20:45.270 --> 00:20:47.790
design or remixing
Daniel and Serena's

00:20:47.790 --> 00:20:50.580
open educational resources
in your own teaching

00:20:50.580 --> 00:20:53.850
head on over to our MIT
OpenCourseWare website.

00:20:53.850 --> 00:20:56.760
You'll find all the
materials there.

00:20:56.760 --> 00:20:58.870
Thank you so much for listening.

00:20:58.870 --> 00:21:02.220
Until next time, signing off
from Cambridge, Massachusetts,

00:21:02.220 --> 00:21:04.470
and about to go
google "pterodactyl,"

00:21:04.470 --> 00:21:07.680
I'm your host, Sarah Hansen,
from MIT OpenCourseWare.

